WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:02.290
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:03.290
My name is Martin Cousins.

3
00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.650
I'm going to be your instructor for this course,

4
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.369
Introduction into Gameplay in Unreal Engine for Programmers

5
00:00:08.369 --> 00:00:09.970
for Unreal 5.5.

6
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:11.810
This is a follow-on from the first course

7
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.970
that we did, which was Introduction to Unreal.

8
00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.489
Quite obviously from the title,

9
00:00:16.489 --> 00:00:18.530
we're going to focus more on the gameplay

10
00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.750
aspects of the engine right now.

11
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.289
So just covering what we're actually

12
00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:27.109
going to go over in the duration of this course,

13
00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:29.429
we've got to do a quick recap of a little bit of what

14
00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:31.750
we covered towards the end of our first course,

15
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.350
just talking a bit about how the fundamental types in the engine.

16
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.829
Then we're going to go more on to the first entry

17
00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:41.950
point for key classes relating to the player

18
00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.509
and some of the gameplay framework,

19
00:00:44.509 --> 00:00:46.670
it would officially be called, that's involved there.

20
00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.390
Going to move on from that a little bit

21
00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.890
and broaden that out a little bit

22
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.109
to talk about game world and the flow that

23
00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:54.030
goes on when you start a game.

24
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.549
Then we're going to talk about animation and inputs,

25
00:00:56.549 --> 00:00:59.750
both obviously very relevant for gameplay code.

26
00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:01.189
And then we're going to finish off

27
00:01:01.189 --> 00:01:05.870
with a couple of sections about just general coding concepts

28
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.349
that are important to be aware of.

29
00:01:07.349 --> 00:01:09.069
You'll probably have encountered things

30
00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:11.590
like this in other domains, but just how Unreal

31
00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:12.629
works with these things.

32
00:01:12.629 --> 00:01:15.310
So that's delegates and events, serialization,

33
00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.549
programming subsystems, which is a more Unreal-specific thing

34
00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:20.890
to be aware of, and then just some of the libraries available

35
00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.909
to you that can just provide some out-of-the-box functionality

36
00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:29.040
to make things a little bit quicker.

37
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.760
So first off, a little bit of a recap

38
00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.129
of what we covered in our last session.

39
00:01:34.129 --> 00:01:38.170
So the fundamental classes on the absolute most base level

40
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.450
on Unreal, we start with UObject.

41
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.129
We'll get into each of these in a little bit,

42
00:01:43.129 --> 00:01:44.250
but we've got UObject.

43
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.890
Inheriting from that, we have actor and actor component.

44
00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.450
And then inheriting from that, we have scene component.

45
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.209
We'll go into a little bit more detail.

46
00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:53.790
UObject is the base class for all our other Unreal

47
00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:54.290
classes.

48
00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:58.329
It's the entry point for reflection.

49
00:01:58.329 --> 00:02:00.569
It's got no physical representation in world,

50
00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:03.689
so most objects that inherit directly from UObject

51
00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:07.569
are kind of intended to be data only.

52
00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:09.449
They have no inbuilt tick functionality,

53
00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.689
tick being the updates.

54
00:02:12.689 --> 00:02:14.490
If your game is running 60 times a second,

55
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.849
your tick would be called 60 times a second.

56
00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:20.289
And UObjects have no inbuilt tick functionality.

57
00:02:20.289 --> 00:02:21.849
When you're spawning UObject, you

58
00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:23.629
have to provide it with an outer, which

59
00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:24.930
is the thing that owns it.

60
00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.409
If you don't provide an outer,

61
00:02:26.409 --> 00:02:28.689
then we use the transient package,

62
00:02:28.689 --> 00:02:31.090
which is essentially things that are not saved.

63
00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.370
So it's a kind of temporary transient object

64
00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.610
that exists for probably the duration of a level.

65
00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.689
UObjects can support networking, but it's not.

66
00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:41.969
You need to do some kind of additional layers

67
00:02:41.969 --> 00:02:42.930
of functionality.

68
00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.009
So if you're intending on doing networking,

69
00:02:45.009 --> 00:02:47.569
UObjects might not always be your first protocol

70
00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:49.969
or shouldn't necessarily be always your first protocol.

71
00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:52.650
And what we gave an example of in our last session

72
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.810
was just a data asset, which is quite a typical way

73
00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:57.969
to use a UObject, which is say

74
00:02:57.969 --> 00:02:59.250
we're trying to define an explosion,

75
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.050
we might have a UObject that has some data that kind of groups

76
00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:07.050
that data together in a kind of editor friendly way.

77
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:10.250
So we might have a particle explosion and a radius,

78
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.330
and we use all of those to define an explosion.

79
00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.939
Then we use that later elsewhere.

80
00:03:15.939 --> 00:03:18.219
Actor inherits from UObject.

81
00:03:18.219 --> 00:03:20.860
And this is kind of another big branching point for the engine

82
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.259
where a lot of things then extend from Actor.

83
00:03:23.259 --> 00:03:25.419
That covers a lot of the gameplay functionality

84
00:03:25.419 --> 00:03:28.060
we'll inherit from Actor.

85
00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:32.699
Unlike UObjects, Actors do provide kind of easy functionality

86
00:03:32.699 --> 00:03:35.139
for replication and connecting between client and server.

87
00:03:35.139 --> 00:03:39.539
So it's whereas you should be a little bit more considerate

88
00:03:39.539 --> 00:03:42.419
when you're trying to implement networked UObjects.

89
00:03:42.419 --> 00:03:47.419
Once you're at the Actor level, it's a lot simpler.

90
00:03:47.979 --> 00:03:49.860
We talked a little bit about this in the last session,

91
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.580
but when things inherit from the UObjects,

92
00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.419
they start with this prefix U,

93
00:03:54.419 --> 00:03:56.900
when they inherit from Actor and downwards from that,

94
00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:58.180
they start with the prefix A,

95
00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.900
and that is hard coded into Unreal.

96
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.259
You'll get compile complaints

97
00:04:02.259 --> 00:04:06.219
if you're not obeying that naming convention.

98
00:04:06.219 --> 00:04:07.620
The other thing about Actors,

99
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.259
which you can kind of see from the display here,

100
00:04:10.259 --> 00:04:13.979
is that Actors are intended to utilize

101
00:04:13.979 --> 00:04:15.979
a kind of component architecture.

102
00:04:15.979 --> 00:04:18.699
So an Actor might inherit

103
00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:21.060
and implement a lot of functionality itself,

104
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.579
but they are also intended to utilize

105
00:04:23.579 --> 00:04:26.339
this kind of modular framework of components

106
00:04:26.779 --> 00:04:30.300
so that it's easy to import kind of similar sets

107
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.860
of functionality between multiple Actors.

108
00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:35.500
Again, referencing our last talk,

109
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:40.500
we had the explosive barrel, which was our example Actor.

110
00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.860
And as you can see, that utilizes several components.

111
00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.339
We've got a scene component, which is our scene route.

112
00:04:45.339 --> 00:04:47.579
I'll talk a little bit more about that again in a second.

113
00:04:47.579 --> 00:04:50.660
Static mesh Actor, explosion component,

114
00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.459
which is our custom component that we made,

115
00:04:52.459 --> 00:04:53.579
and then just some variables.

116
00:04:53.579 --> 00:04:56.899
So Actors can implement their own functionality,

117
00:04:56.899 --> 00:04:59.420
but they're best used as kind of ways

118
00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.899
to combine sets of functionality through components.

119
00:05:03.899 --> 00:05:06.860
Components, again, we've already covered a little bit,

120
00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.660
but they are essentially our modular building block.

121
00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.259
So, you know, typical out of the box examples

122
00:05:13.259 --> 00:05:17.100
would be a collision component or a mesh component.

123
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.139
So if you want to be able to visualize something

124
00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:20.300
or provide collision,

125
00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.740
those are really, really obvious examples.

126
00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.660
Another one I can think of off the top of my head

127
00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.300
is something that provides just pure functionality,

128
00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.259
something like a path following component,

129
00:05:31.259 --> 00:05:32.620
or I think there's a component

130
00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.540
just to make things spin on the spot.

131
00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:36.100
If you just need, I don't know,

132
00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.939
a display in a gallery that just spins,

133
00:05:37.939 --> 00:05:39.660
there's a component for that.

134
00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.540
It provides this functionality in a portable way

135
00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.100
that you can easily chop and change it

136
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.139
and put it on different Actors.

137
00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:47.939
There is a relatively strict relationship

138
00:05:47.939 --> 00:05:49.259
between components and Actors

139
00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:52.379
in that components can't exist without Actors.

140
00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:54.620
Actors contain their components

141
00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.459
and that kind of, it's not a one-to-one relationship

142
00:05:57.459 --> 00:05:59.100
because an Actor can have many components,

143
00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.939
but a current component cannot exist without an Actor.

144
00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:04.939
Yeah, and essentially they provide

145
00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:07.420
that kind of back and forth communication between them.

146
00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.540
Components are aware of the Actor that owns them

147
00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.779
and the Actor is aware of the components that it has,

148
00:06:11.779 --> 00:06:13.420
which hence what this blue arrow is there

149
00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.379
to kind of communicate.

150
00:06:15.379 --> 00:06:18.220
And they kind of step down from component

151
00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.019
from just a plain Actor component is the scene component.

152
00:06:21.019 --> 00:06:25.139
This is inherits, hence this kind of line to indicate that.

153
00:06:25.139 --> 00:06:28.660
And a scene component is essentially an Actor component,

154
00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:31.100
but it has physical presence in the level.

155
00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.100
Where an Actor component can just provide functionality,

156
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.459
scene components also provide functionality,

157
00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:39.019
but in the context of a position in world.

158
00:06:39.019 --> 00:06:42.139
So when I gave the example of a collision

159
00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:45.420
or a visual mesh, obviously both of those things

160
00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:46.740
would have positions in world.

161
00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.939
So both of those things would actually inherit

162
00:06:48.939 --> 00:06:50.779
from a scene component.

163
00:06:50.779 --> 00:06:54.060
Actors also require, at least at the blueprint level,

164
00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.899
require at least one scene component,

165
00:06:55.899 --> 00:06:58.740
which is referred to as the root component.

166
00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.060
And when you ask an Actor,

167
00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.300
what is your location or rotation of world?

168
00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.660
Actually that Actor doesn't innately

169
00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:06.980
have a location and rotation.

170
00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:08.939
What you're actually asking is what are the,

171
00:07:08.939 --> 00:07:10.259
is the location and rotation

172
00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:12.180
of that Actor's root component.

173
00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.860
They can only have one root component

174
00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.019
and then their other components

175
00:07:15.019 --> 00:07:16.899
are attached to that component.

176
00:07:16.899 --> 00:07:20.860
And scene components have this kind of hierarchical,

177
00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:21.939
unlike Actor components,

178
00:07:21.939 --> 00:07:23.579
which are kind of just slotted in,

179
00:07:23.579 --> 00:07:25.420
scene components have a hierarchy between them.

180
00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:26.819
So you can child them to each other

181
00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:29.379
and all scene components of an Actor

182
00:07:29.379 --> 00:07:32.449
are childed to the root component.

183
00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:34.529
So that about covers our kind of fundamentals.

184
00:07:34.529 --> 00:07:36.649
Again, if you want to go into any more detail,

185
00:07:36.649 --> 00:07:39.209
any of that, you can go into our previous talk,

186
00:07:39.209 --> 00:07:40.850
but now let's talk a little bit more

187
00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.290
about the kind of the key classes

188
00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:47.740
relating to the player.

189
00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:49.300
So once we get to this level,

190
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.819
we've got quite a bit of a more complex

191
00:07:51.819 --> 00:07:54.579
web of relationships to be aware of,

192
00:07:54.579 --> 00:07:57.980
hence the kind of slightly possibly overwhelming graph.

193
00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.819
Again, just to highlight what all these visuals mean,

194
00:08:01.819 --> 00:08:03.540
we have these kind of solid white lines.

195
00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.819
This is just to kind of demonstrate inheritance

196
00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:07.899
and the dotted white lines is just indicating,

197
00:08:07.899 --> 00:08:09.579
obviously there are a lot more classes

198
00:08:09.579 --> 00:08:10.699
that inherit from Actor.

199
00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:13.019
There are a lot more classes that inherit from Pawn

200
00:08:13.019 --> 00:08:14.980
and so on and so forth.

201
00:08:15.019 --> 00:08:18.019
But this is the kind of things

202
00:08:18.019 --> 00:08:19.420
we're going to go into right now.

203
00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:20.660
These are the kind of relationships

204
00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:21.620
we need to be concerned about.

205
00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.860
We'll zoom into each of these as we go along

206
00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.259
and talk a little bit more about them,

207
00:08:25.259 --> 00:08:27.019
but this is just kind of an overview

208
00:08:27.019 --> 00:08:29.930
of all the things we're going to be covering.

209
00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:31.730
So we already talked about Actor,

210
00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:33.370
but once we're at the player level,

211
00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.529
one of the first things to talk about is a Pawn.

212
00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:39.529
A Pawn is essentially a base class of any Actor

213
00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.090
that can be possessed.

214
00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.769
That is either be by a player or an AI.

215
00:08:44.769 --> 00:08:46.889
Possess is the term for this thing

216
00:08:46.889 --> 00:08:49.210
has something controlling it.

217
00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:53.450
A Pawn is the physical representation of a player and agent.

218
00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.289
So it exists in world.

219
00:08:55.289 --> 00:08:57.450
Typically when you have a playable character,

220
00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.610
it would be a Pawn.

221
00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.690
And it's kind of a very basic plain canvas.

222
00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.929
It doesn't innately have much in the way

223
00:09:04.929 --> 00:09:06.049
of movement capability.

224
00:09:06.049 --> 00:09:07.129
It doesn't have collision.

225
00:09:07.129 --> 00:09:09.090
It has no visuals.

226
00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:11.490
A basic Pawn has very, very little in it.

227
00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.769
Just this functionality to be controlled.

228
00:09:14.769 --> 00:09:17.450
Pawns have a one-to-one relationship with a controller.

229
00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:19.730
Hence this kind of blue arrow here.

230
00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.649
Pawns are aware of the thing that controls them,

231
00:09:21.649 --> 00:09:23.610
which is a controller, which we'll touch on in a second.

232
00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.970
And controllers are aware of the Pawn they're controlling.

233
00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:28.809
Pawns aren't assumed to be humanoid.

234
00:09:28.809 --> 00:09:29.730
They could be anything.

235
00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:31.450
They could be abstract.

236
00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:33.129
They do have a location in world,

237
00:09:33.129 --> 00:09:36.649
but you could have a Pawn that represents a mini game.

238
00:09:36.649 --> 00:09:38.129
And it's kind of like the thing

239
00:09:38.129 --> 00:09:39.850
that's pulling all your input together.

240
00:09:39.889 --> 00:09:43.049
You could have a Pawn that represents a flying drone

241
00:09:43.049 --> 00:09:43.889
or something like that.

242
00:09:43.889 --> 00:09:45.610
It's not inherently a character

243
00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:48.570
with two legs running around kind of thing.

244
00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:49.970
It's also worth touching on the fact

245
00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.809
that Actors themselves can consume input

246
00:09:53.809 --> 00:09:56.450
and have their transform manipulated.

247
00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:58.570
So that is not unique to Pawn,

248
00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:01.009
but it is facilitated more strongly within Pawn.

249
00:10:01.009 --> 00:10:03.529
So if you are thinking about controlling something,

250
00:10:03.529 --> 00:10:05.129
Pawn should be your starting point.

251
00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:07.889
It's Actors you can listen for input,

252
00:10:07.889 --> 00:10:09.090
but it requires a bit more setup

253
00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:12.779
and that should be for special cases really.

254
00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:15.179
So coming down from the Pawn level,

255
00:10:15.179 --> 00:10:16.419
we have two more classes.

256
00:10:16.419 --> 00:10:18.700
We have Default Pawn and Spectator Pawn.

257
00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:22.179
Default Pawn is not inherited from

258
00:10:22.179 --> 00:10:24.379
in other classes from the game.

259
00:10:24.379 --> 00:10:26.299
It's only into Spectator Pawn.

260
00:10:26.299 --> 00:10:28.100
So it should not be the base class

261
00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:29.860
for when you're intending to make your own.

262
00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.899
It's just the kind of engine Default Pawn.

263
00:10:31.899 --> 00:10:33.820
So if you're in a random level

264
00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:36.259
and you don't have anything playable

265
00:10:36.259 --> 00:10:38.299
and you just click play,

266
00:10:38.299 --> 00:10:40.740
I'm currently playing as the Default Pawn now.

267
00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.940
It essentially provides some basic movement functionality.

268
00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.820
It does have a collision.

269
00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.500
It's got no gravity.

270
00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.019
It's kind of a flying style movement

271
00:10:50.019 --> 00:10:52.379
and it's bound to input.

272
00:10:52.379 --> 00:10:55.139
So by default, you can use WASD

273
00:10:55.139 --> 00:10:57.710
and jumping to kind of move up.

274
00:10:57.710 --> 00:10:59.389
And it just provides some really,

275
00:10:59.389 --> 00:11:01.990
really basic fundamental functionality.

276
00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:04.750
Spectator Pawn inherits from Default Pawn

277
00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:08.309
and therefore brings along a lot of the same functionality.

278
00:11:08.309 --> 00:11:10.110
Actually what the Spectator Pawn does

279
00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.870
is replace the movement component that it uses.

280
00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:16.110
The Spectator Pawn's movement component

281
00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.269
is primarily there to add in some extra functionality

282
00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:21.070
for the ability to possibly ignore time dilation

283
00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:23.750
if you don't want slow-mo to affect it.

284
00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.110
And also just to remove the concept of a mesh

285
00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:28.710
because the spectators are meant to be abstract.

286
00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.269
A spectator obviously being for

287
00:11:30.269 --> 00:11:32.269
at the end of a match if your character has died

288
00:11:32.269 --> 00:11:34.509
and you still want to move around the space,

289
00:11:34.509 --> 00:11:35.909
that's the kind of assumption

290
00:11:36.309 --> 00:11:38.590
for why the Spectator Pawn exists.

291
00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:40.389
Obviously if you want to implement your own,

292
00:11:40.389 --> 00:11:43.529
you can absolutely do that.

293
00:11:43.529 --> 00:11:45.649
So going back up that chain slightly,

294
00:11:45.649 --> 00:11:47.370
the other class that we have out of the box

295
00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.330
inheriting from Pawn that we need to talk about

296
00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:50.610
is the Character.

297
00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.929
Character is essentially a shortcut class.

298
00:11:52.929 --> 00:11:54.809
You don't need to use it,

299
00:11:54.809 --> 00:11:56.889
but essentially what Epic realized

300
00:11:56.889 --> 00:11:58.850
was the vast majority of the time

301
00:11:58.850 --> 00:12:00.090
when we are making games

302
00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:01.730
or if we've got a playable character,

303
00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:04.970
it is typically an upright,

304
00:12:05.009 --> 00:12:07.730
probably capsule-like shape

305
00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.210
and we would need movement,

306
00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:12.690
we would need visual mesh and we would need collision.

307
00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:15.129
So the character essentially just provides

308
00:12:15.129 --> 00:12:16.490
all of that stuff out of the box.

309
00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:18.289
It comes with a collision component,

310
00:12:18.289 --> 00:12:20.090
it comes with a skeletal mesh component

311
00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:22.970
because most of the time our characters are animated

312
00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:25.330
and it also comes with movement functionality

313
00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:26.169
baked into it.

314
00:12:26.169 --> 00:12:27.409
It has this special relationship

315
00:12:27.409 --> 00:12:29.129
with a unique kind of movement component

316
00:12:29.129 --> 00:12:30.730
called a character movement component,

317
00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.370
which we'll get into in a moment.

318
00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:35.370
But typically when you're making a human-like

319
00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.169
or humanoid character,

320
00:12:37.169 --> 00:12:39.169
a character might as well be your starting point

321
00:12:39.169 --> 00:12:42.149
because it just provides a bunch of functionality for you.

322
00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:44.870
The character movement component is a specialized form

323
00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:46.429
of the Pawn movement component

324
00:12:46.429 --> 00:12:48.590
and it's linked quite tightly with the character.

325
00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:51.269
So every time you pull in a character class,

326
00:12:51.269 --> 00:12:52.149
it's always going to pull in

327
00:12:52.149 --> 00:12:53.789
a character movement component with it

328
00:12:53.789 --> 00:12:55.389
and the character movement component

329
00:12:55.389 --> 00:12:57.149
depends on a character existing.

330
00:12:57.149 --> 00:12:59.509
It expects to be implementing movement

331
00:12:59.509 --> 00:13:02.110
for a character class.

332
00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:04.230
The movement component provides

333
00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:05.870
a whole bunch of functionality.

334
00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.509
It's first and foremost,

335
00:13:07.509 --> 00:13:09.870
it's got networking already baked into it,

336
00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.429
meaning that if you just instantly create

337
00:13:12.429 --> 00:13:15.789
a networked multiplayer game and you're using characters,

338
00:13:15.789 --> 00:13:18.070
that will mean you've already got your positions

339
00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:19.350
and rotations being communicated

340
00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:20.710
between the server and client.

341
00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.110
And it means that you've got client-side prediction,

342
00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:24.110
meaning that you're going to get

343
00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:26.269
a less aggressive reconciliation

344
00:13:26.269 --> 00:13:27.990
between your server and your client.

345
00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:30.710
It accounts for several different modes of moving.

346
00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:33.909
So we have walking, running, falling, swimming, and flying.

347
00:13:35.590 --> 00:13:37.909
And each of these is kind of handled distinctly

348
00:13:37.909 --> 00:13:38.750
within the code.

349
00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.429
So if you need to kind of investigate

350
00:13:41.429 --> 00:13:43.789
or inject functionality into these classes,

351
00:13:43.789 --> 00:13:46.710
they're all kind of broken up into separate sections.

352
00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:49.509
You can also add in additional movement modes.

353
00:13:49.509 --> 00:13:50.830
So if you want to add in

354
00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:52.950
your own custom new movement mode

355
00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:55.110
that doesn't fall under any of the preexisting categories,

356
00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:56.710
you can do that.

357
00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:59.950
That being said, the character movement component

358
00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:01.789
isn't marked as blueprintable,

359
00:14:01.789 --> 00:14:03.750
so you can't make blueprints that inherit

360
00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:06.029
from the character movement component by default,

361
00:14:06.029 --> 00:14:08.870
unless you can go and edit the engine code yourself.

362
00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:10.909
And what that means is that if you do want

363
00:14:10.909 --> 00:14:14.870
to kind of blueprint your own custom movement mode,

364
00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:16.470
the way that's actually best to do that

365
00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:19.190
is on the character blueprint itself.

366
00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:20.710
So because the character

367
00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:23.549
and the character movement component are so tightly linked,

368
00:14:23.549 --> 00:14:26.110
quite a lot of the movement functionality

369
00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:28.309
can be accessed through the character class.

370
00:14:28.309 --> 00:14:32.190
And so the update custom movement event

371
00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:35.710
on the character blueprint itself would be getting called,

372
00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:37.870
and that's the point where you can inject

373
00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.750
any kind of custom movement functionality that you need.

374
00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:42.070
The other thing to kind of bear in mind

375
00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:44.429
is that as character is, you know,

376
00:14:44.429 --> 00:14:47.429
inherently got a capsule, an upright standing capsule,

377
00:14:47.429 --> 00:14:49.710
character movement component is expecting you

378
00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:53.190
to make an upright vertically aligned character.

379
00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.389
So if you're not doing something like that

380
00:14:55.389 --> 00:14:56.669
and you want something more like a,

381
00:14:56.669 --> 00:14:59.750
maybe like, I don't know, something quadrupedal,

382
00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:02.429
like a dog or a cat character

383
00:15:02.429 --> 00:15:03.429
and the character movement component

384
00:15:03.429 --> 00:15:07.379
might not be completely suitable for that.

385
00:15:07.379 --> 00:15:09.419
So stepping all the way back up the chain now

386
00:15:09.419 --> 00:15:11.259
up to actor and inheriting from that,

387
00:15:11.259 --> 00:15:12.379
we have controllers.

388
00:15:12.379 --> 00:15:14.860
We've touched on in this a little bit already,

389
00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.419
but controllers are essentially

390
00:15:17.419 --> 00:15:20.179
where a pawn is the thing that gets controlled.

391
00:15:20.179 --> 00:15:22.299
A controller is the thing that does the controlling.

392
00:15:22.299 --> 00:15:23.659
So where a pawn represents

393
00:15:23.659 --> 00:15:26.659
the kind of physical object in world,

394
00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:29.139
controller represents a little bit more

395
00:15:29.139 --> 00:15:32.299
like that object's mind.

396
00:15:32.299 --> 00:15:33.779
It has no physical actor.

397
00:15:33.779 --> 00:15:35.860
It does technically have a position in world.

398
00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:39.139
Like if you started play, if I played a level

399
00:15:39.139 --> 00:15:43.659
and you could actually see that I do have a,

400
00:15:43.659 --> 00:15:45.419
there are controllers actually in my level

401
00:15:45.419 --> 00:15:47.419
and they do actually have a location,

402
00:15:47.419 --> 00:15:49.620
but for the most part,

403
00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:51.700
their transform is somewhat irrelevant.

404
00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:53.740
They don't have any kind of collision

405
00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:56.019
or visual representation.

406
00:15:56.019 --> 00:15:59.299
The main purpose for a controller is to possess a pawn.

407
00:16:00.419 --> 00:16:02.340
It has a one-to-one relationship with a pawn.

408
00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:05.860
So a pawn can only be controlled by one controller.

409
00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:09.419
Really it's intended that controllers

410
00:16:09.419 --> 00:16:11.299
only control one pawn.

411
00:16:11.299 --> 00:16:13.740
Obviously in certain situations and certain game setups

412
00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.659
that might not be completely suitable,

413
00:16:15.659 --> 00:16:18.179
but the standard intent is for a controller

414
00:16:18.179 --> 00:16:19.220
to control a single pawn

415
00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:23.110
and a pawn to be controlled by a single controller.

416
00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:25.629
Controllers also get informed about a lot of events

417
00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:27.750
and notifications about the pawns they're controlling,

418
00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:29.590
if they possess it, if they stop possessing it

419
00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:31.269
and many other things.

420
00:16:31.269 --> 00:16:32.590
The other thing is that

421
00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:33.870
none of the transform functionalities

422
00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:36.710
are exposed in native in C++.

423
00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:41.110
So again, you're really not intended to manipulate

424
00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:43.269
the location and rotation of the controller.

425
00:16:43.269 --> 00:16:45.350
One thing to talk about very briefly though,

426
00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:46.669
is that as you could see

427
00:16:46.669 --> 00:16:49.029
when I was quickly demonstrating there,

428
00:16:49.029 --> 00:16:53.029
if at runtime you have your controller selected,

429
00:16:53.029 --> 00:16:57.230
you will be able to see that our rotation is changing

430
00:16:57.230 --> 00:16:59.549
as we move our viewer around.

431
00:16:59.549 --> 00:17:01.509
This is kind of a slightly more in depth,

432
00:17:01.509 --> 00:17:05.069
but essentially when you are controlling a pawn,

433
00:17:05.069 --> 00:17:08.150
you have a concept called the control rotation.

434
00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:10.990
And that is vaguely meant a way,

435
00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:12.710
a default way of representing

436
00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:16.430
what is the kind of intent direction of this pawn.

437
00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:18.430
And that might be the player's view rotation.

438
00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:21.230
It might be the direction that an AI is moving in.

439
00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:22.869
And that is when you're talking about

440
00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:23.710
that control rotation,

441
00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:26.950
that is actually the rotation of the controller.

442
00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:28.910
So whilst for the most part,

443
00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:31.349
you don't need to be editing these things,

444
00:17:31.349 --> 00:17:33.589
the rotation of the controller

445
00:17:33.589 --> 00:17:34.990
does actually have some meaning,

446
00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:38.880
which is why you see updating in runtime.

447
00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:41.160
So inheriting from controllers themselves,

448
00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:42.680
we have two different classes.

449
00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:44.839
Unlike these other classes, pawn and actor,

450
00:17:44.839 --> 00:17:46.880
which might have a lot of things inheriting from them,

451
00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:49.319
controller is specifically only inherited

452
00:17:49.319 --> 00:17:50.480
by these two types.

453
00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.440
You could inherit from it yourself,

454
00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.920
but a controller is an abstract class

455
00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:57.000
and player controller and AI controller,

456
00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:58.960
where we actually kind of get into things

457
00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:02.160
that you might actually see getting spawned in your game.

458
00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:06.440
So a player controller represents the human player's will.

459
00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:09.240
You would have a player controller exist

460
00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:12.279
for every human player in your game.

461
00:18:12.279 --> 00:18:17.039
So if you had a local co-op game

462
00:18:17.039 --> 00:18:18.359
and you had four players,

463
00:18:18.359 --> 00:18:20.599
you would expect to have four player controllers

464
00:18:20.599 --> 00:18:21.839
spawned in your level.

465
00:18:22.599 --> 00:18:24.599
They essentially act as a centralizing point

466
00:18:24.599 --> 00:18:26.759
for your player's camera and input.

467
00:18:26.759 --> 00:18:29.000
Both of these systems are actually handled

468
00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:31.319
by other systems under the hood.

469
00:18:31.319 --> 00:18:32.880
There's something called the player camera manager

470
00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.279
and the player input components,

471
00:18:34.279 --> 00:18:36.160
which they kind of really handle

472
00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:37.759
each of those things directly,

473
00:18:37.759 --> 00:18:39.599
but the player controller gives you a way

474
00:18:39.599 --> 00:18:42.039
to interface with both of those things.

475
00:18:42.039 --> 00:18:43.920
Player controllers also persist

476
00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:45.880
throughout the duration of a world.

477
00:18:47.079 --> 00:18:48.839
That's true of controllers in general,

478
00:18:48.839 --> 00:18:51.880
but typically when you start a level,

479
00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:53.119
your players would already exist.

480
00:18:53.119 --> 00:18:55.720
There is functionality to spawn new players in,

481
00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:57.599
but a player controller would exist

482
00:18:57.599 --> 00:18:58.680
for the duration of a world

483
00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.990
as long as that player exists.

484
00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:03.670
The other thing we'll get onto in a moment

485
00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:05.589
is the fact that the player controller exists

486
00:19:05.589 --> 00:19:08.750
on the server and on the controlling client's machine.

487
00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.869
So in a networked multiplayer scenario,

488
00:19:11.869 --> 00:19:14.109
the server would have a copy of each player controller

489
00:19:14.109 --> 00:19:17.069
and each client would see their own player controller,

490
00:19:17.069 --> 00:19:18.509
but not others.

491
00:19:18.509 --> 00:19:20.309
We'll go into more detail about that later,

492
00:19:20.309 --> 00:19:24.089
but it's a thing that's worth highlighting.

493
00:19:24.089 --> 00:19:25.410
So separately from that,

494
00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:27.769
the other kind of branch that we can go down

495
00:19:27.769 --> 00:19:29.569
is AI controllers.

496
00:19:29.569 --> 00:19:30.730
Pretty self-explanatory.

497
00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:35.730
This is the will of an AI entity.

498
00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:40.730
So where a player controller represents a human player,

499
00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:43.769
an AI controller represents some kind of AI

500
00:19:43.769 --> 00:19:46.210
that would be controlling entities within the world.

501
00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.410
Just like a player controller,

502
00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:49.250
because they're a form of controller,

503
00:19:49.250 --> 00:19:51.009
they can possess pawns.

504
00:19:51.009 --> 00:19:53.529
And this means that you can have a pawn

505
00:19:53.529 --> 00:19:55.049
that can be controlled by a player

506
00:19:55.049 --> 00:19:58.089
and the exact same pawn could be controlled by an AI.

507
00:19:58.089 --> 00:20:00.450
And the idea being that it's intentionally abstracted

508
00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:01.769
into separate sections

509
00:20:01.769 --> 00:20:04.170
so that you might be able to reuse these things,

510
00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:07.130
thinking about something like a game like Unreal Tournament

511
00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:07.970
or something like that.

512
00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:10.210
You really have the same characters running around,

513
00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:12.970
picking up guns, driving cars, shooting things.

514
00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:15.769
And it might be a human controlling that entity

515
00:20:15.769 --> 00:20:16.769
or it might be an AI.

516
00:20:16.809 --> 00:20:19.329
So this is kind of helpful way to think about these things,

517
00:20:19.329 --> 00:20:22.309
about why they might be structured the way they are.

518
00:20:22.309 --> 00:20:24.430
And the way an AI controller works

519
00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:26.150
is there's a set of components

520
00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.589
that really kind of drive that logic.

521
00:20:28.589 --> 00:20:30.869
We'll get into more detail in this in another session,

522
00:20:30.869 --> 00:20:32.589
but just briefly to touch on it,

523
00:20:32.589 --> 00:20:34.230
you have the blackboard component,

524
00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:38.430
which is essentially that AI's kind of knowledge and memory.

525
00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:39.829
And then you have the brain component,

526
00:20:39.829 --> 00:20:41.990
which kind of performs the logic.

527
00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:44.230
And that's most typically exists

528
00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:46.869
in the form of the behavior tree,

529
00:20:46.869 --> 00:20:49.230
but there are other classes that implement that behavior,

530
00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.829
that kind of brain logic.

531
00:20:51.829 --> 00:20:54.390
The other thing to highlight is unlike player controllers,

532
00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:55.910
these only exist on the server

533
00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:59.990
because Unreal is server authoritative.

534
